A package has nothing to name and a user has no way to say what they are
running. Both blockers 29 found are down to this one: the licence is settled
(30) and this is the other.

  - a VERSION line at the top of bin/pidly, which a vendored copy carries
  - pidly --version, printing it
  - a tag, a tarball with a stable URL, and a checksum

No build step, no compilation, nothing beyond a POSIX shell, the usual
coreutils and an optional git.

0.x, not 1.0: 28 has landed but the tap and the AUR have not, and 29 says not
to call anything 1.0 before the schema file on disk has settled in the hands
of someone who did not write it.

The release itself — tagging and publishing — is the owner's, not an agent's.

Done when the script carries a version, --version prints it, the suite covers
both, and the steps to cut a release are written down where the next person
will find them.


Done 2026-09-18, except the release itself, which is the owner's.

  VERSION=0.1.0 at the top of bin/pidly, beside the SPDX line and for the
  same reason: a copy vendored into another repository is the whole of pidly
  and has to answer for itself.

  pidly --version, and -V, printing "pidly 0.1.0".

Nothing on disk records it. A tracker carries no version and must not, which
is 32's reasoning and the same sentence appears there: a version is state
that can disagree with the files.

--version answers before anything else does — outside a tracker, where every
other command refuses, and on a first install before init has run. That is
the case it exists for, so it is the case the suite pins.

The release steps are in README.md under "Releasing", next to a new
"Installing" section carrying the curl fallback 29 asked to have written down
whatever else happened. A release is a tag and the script at that tag: bump
VERSION, regenerate the two documents that quote the usage text, run check
and the suite, tag, and take the checksum of the tarball the forge builds.
Tagging and pushing are not an agent's to do.

Also corrected README's Status section, which listed thirteen commands and
had missed set, needs, fields, reparent, field, show, claude and man.

tests/version.sh covers it, and the assertion worth naming is that what the
flag prints is read back out of the script's own VERSION line: a literal in
the printf would pass every other test here and lie after the next bump.

The man page picked the flag up for free, since it is generated from the
usage text.

Not done here, and deliberately: 31 decided the manual page carries no
version, and that decision still holds. A .TH version would put the page out
of date at every bump, which is a release step rather than a fault, but it is
31's call to revisit and not this ticket's.
